182
00:17:03,570 --> 00:17:04,788
Rym: Guess what never happens?

183
00:17:05,372 --> 00:17:06,608
Scott: I don't think that really happened.

184
00:17:07,310 --> 00:17:14,069
Rym: I remember we tried to make it happen, but a lot of games didn't... Basically, we always tried to do this at conventions, and we'd always have problems with it.

185
00:17:14,931 --> 00:17:21,530
Scott: Yeah, but I guess the Switch succeeded because it was portable and also not portable at the same time.

186
00:17:21,951 --> 00:17:22,011
Rym: Yep.

187
00:17:22,151 --> 00:17:23,315
Rym: Also, digital.

188
00:17:24,238 --> 00:17:27,589
Rym: All the indications are that nobody wants to buy these physical cartridges anymore.

189
00:17:27,790 --> 00:17:33,290
Scott: Yeah, I wonder if the next one will have a physical game cartridge or anything at all.

190
00:17:33,410 --> 00:17:38,299
Rym: I wouldn't be surprised if it didn't, but that would imply that this is the point.

191
00:17:38,419 --> 00:17:44,250
Rym: where Switch onward is when Nintendo has to maintain seem-like levels of backward compatibility.

192
00:17:45,811 --> 00:18:06,890
Scott: There has been very, very strong indications and implications, even from Nintendo, talking about their accounts, how basically we're almost guaranteed, almost, nothing's truly guaranteed, that at least the next console, you'll log in with your Nintendo account, and the games that you bought on Switch will be there and will work, mostly.

193
00:18:07,611 --> 00:18:12,688
Scott: As far as we can tell, something like that along those lines is going to happen, so...

194
00:18:14,190 --> 00:18:27,710
Rym: So, in some other news, Helldivers 2, which I was excited about until I wasn't, is having a few things going on in that, one, it is so stupidly popular that no one can get into servers, and it's like crashing and having all sorts of...

195
00:18:27,730 --> 00:18:29,190
Scott: Maybe you should let people run their own servers.

196
00:18:31,050 --> 00:18:47,070
Rym: But also, it's having lots of bugs and issues that seem to be related to onerous anti-cheat technology that is built into it, and this is notable for one specific reason that is relevant to our main bit, and it's actually the reason why...

197
00:18:47,330 --> 00:18:48,849
Scott: If you play on PS5, you don't have to deal with that, I guess.

198
00:18:49,231 --> 00:18:52,429
Rym: True, though people are saying the PS5 is having the most problems.

199
00:18:53,450 --> 00:19:05,770
Scott: Interesting, because it was like, I thought the whole point of this game was like, it's not a PS5 exclusive, because it's on PC, obviously, but it was like, I think they sold it more as a PS5 game, and were like, oh yeah, it's also on PC.

200
00:19:06,290 --> 00:19:12,650
Rym: I mean, Fortnite has cross-play, but the PC people just play with PC people, and that's not even a thing to them.

201
00:19:14,411 --> 00:19:18,918
Rym: So, Helldivers 2 has fairly extremely onerous anti-cheat.

202
00:19:18,938 --> 00:19:26,990
Rym: that a lot of people are complaining very vocally online is being tripped up by Windows Defender, even, as malware.

203
00:19:28,670 --> 00:19:38,270
Rym: And this is weird, because Helldivers is a cooperative PvE game you're intended to play with your friends.

204
00:19:38,970 --> 00:19:45,730
Rym: So why is there Gameguard on a game that is not in any way competitive whatsoever?

205
00:19:46,831 --> 00:19:54,530
Rym: So, not that long ago, actually, February 8th, they actually gave a statement on anti-cheat.

206
00:19:54,850 --> 00:20:07,310
Rym: So, Peter Lindgren, the technical director of Helldivers 2, basically answered a bunch of questions about the anti-cheat, and I'm going to link to it, because there's no point in just reading this whole thing to you, just read it.

207
00:20:07,991 --> 00:20:16,645
Rym: Me summarizing it is not going to help you that much, but one of the questions is, and I'm quoting, "Helldivers 2 is a co-op PvE game.

208
00:20:17,106 --> 00:20:18,368
Rym: Why do we even need

209
00:20:18,488 --> 00:20:19,089
Rym: anti-cheat?"

210
00:20:19,650 --> 00:20:22,016
Rym: And the fact that he starts with, "That's a great question,".

211
00:20:22,036 --> 00:20:28,170
Rym: I have found in my professional experience that usually when someone says, "That's a great question," they're not going to give an answer.

212
00:20:29,191 --> 00:20:30,076
Scott: We know the answer.

213
00:20:30,659 --> 00:20:32,570
Scott: The guy doesn't have the guts to say the real answer.

214
00:20:33,131 --> 00:20:36,460
Scott: If he had the guts, I would just clap, even though he's saying something evil.

215
00:20:36,540 --> 00:20:38,746
Scott: I'd be like, "Bravo for honesty,

216
00:20:38,786 --> 00:20:39,167
Scott: bravo.".

217
00:20:39,407 --> 00:20:40,410
Scott: But he doesn't do that, does he?

218
00:20:40,450 --> 00:20:50,130
Rym: So I'm just going to read all about the first bit of his response, and you can read the rest of this, but first, we want everyone to have a great time playing Helldivers 2 with friends, ex-friends, or randoms.

219
00:20:50,510 --> 00:21:01,210
Rym: What we've seen in some of our and others' games is that rampant cheating tends to have a very negative effect on players' openness to playing, especially with randoms.

220
00:21:03,463 --> 00:21:04,510
Scott: We don't need to play with randoms.

221
00:21:04,790 --> 00:21:08,116
Rym: Yeah, I would never play a PvE game with randoms.

222
00:21:08,156 --> 00:21:15,830
Rym: The entire point of PvE is either I'm going to play it single player, I'm going to play it with the Scots or my other friends.

223
00:21:17,531 --> 00:21:30,610
Rym: I don't really want to play co-operative games with randoms 99% of the time, but we all know the real reason is because of the microtransactions and the advancement nonsense in this game.

224
00:21:31,191 --> 00:21:43,650
Rym: So as much as I want to play Helldivers 2, because all indications are that the game itself is really fun, but the anti-cheat seems so onerous that I think I'm just going to give this one a pass unless something materially changes.

225
00:21:46,932 --> 00:21:49,102
Scott: I think the main thing is the price of it, right?

226
00:21:49,163 --> 00:21:50,590
Scott: It's still an expensive game.

227
00:21:51,710 --> 00:21:53,886
Rym: I mean, I haven't even killed Ganon yet, so...

228
00:21:54,591 --> 00:21:55,257
Scott: Me either.

229
00:21:55,338 --> 00:21:56,629
Scott: I could any time I wanted to.

230
00:21:56,910 --> 00:21:57,433
Rym: I could too.

231
00:21:57,514 --> 00:21:59,990
Rym: There's a few quests I could do, or I could kill Ganon.

232
00:22:00,152 --> 00:22:01,327
Scott: There's a lot of quests I could do.

233
00:22:03,070 --> 00:22:03,930
Rym: There's a few I'm not going to do.

234
00:22:04,870 --> 00:22:10,090
Scott: I mean, it's like I do the game otherwise, other than the anti-cheat thing and the fact that servers aren't working.

235
00:22:10,811 --> 00:22:14,140
Scott: It's actually like, you know, I do kind of want to play that game still, right?

236
00:22:14,280 --> 00:22:17,590
Scott: But I think what's going to happen, if you look, Helldivers 1 is still operating, right?

237
00:22:17,833 --> 00:22:19,470
Scott: And Helldivers 2 is very popular.

238
00:22:20,310 --> 00:22:30,870
Scott: So, and it's because it's a PvE and not a PvP, I don't feel like, even though it's multiplayer, I don't feel like I'm missing out on anything by not playing it immediately.

239
00:22:31,030 --> 00:22:36,666
Rym: Oh yeah, because none of our friends have time to play it anyway, so like three years from now we'll get the game of the year edition and we'll play it.

240
00:22:37,769 --> 00:22:37,970
Scott: Or not.

241
00:22:38,010 --> 00:22:44,970
Scott: What I'm saying is like, yeah, I could just get this later, cheaper, and have all the same exact amount of fun, and there'll be a lot of things patched.

242
00:22:45,130 --> 00:22:48,410
Scott: It'll might even be more fun, and I won't have to suffer through the dark times.

243
00:22:48,610 --> 00:22:48,891
Scott: Yup.

244
00:22:51,175 --> 00:22:54,921
Rym: Isn't using anti-cheat just to protect the game's monetization structure?

245
00:22:55,442 --> 00:22:59,329
Rym: Slightly non-cowardly answer, yes, comma, and no.

246
00:23:01,336 --> 00:23:02,249
Scott: I think it's mostly yes.

247
00:23:02,390 --> 00:23:04,004
Scott: While it will protect monetized content.

248
00:23:05,270 --> 00:23:09,310
Scott: You can't sell someone a health pack if they cheat for infinite health, right?

249
00:23:09,911 --> 00:23:18,707
Rym: I just think back to that Penny Arcade when randos online were mad about people cheating in Diablo, and it was like, who is harmed?

250
00:23:19,047 --> 00:23:19,648
Rym: The devil?

251
00:23:19,668 --> 00:23:20,450
Scott: Fuck him.

252
00:23:21,350 --> 00:23:22,893
Scott: That's right, I'll never forget that one.

253
00:23:22,914 --> 00:23:25,900
Scott: What's wrong with people cheating in Diablo?

254
00:23:25,920 --> 00:23:26,963
Scott: Who's the cheated party?

255
00:23:27,023 --> 00:23:27,504
Scott: The devil?

256
00:23:28,025 --> 00:23:28,807
Scott: Cheating in the devil?

257
00:23:28,867 --> 00:23:29,268
Rym: Fuck him.

258
00:23:30,750 --> 00:23:33,909
Rym: Unless a game is a competitive orthogame, who gives a shit if someone cheats?

259
00:23:34,810 --> 00:23:35,209
Scott: That's right.

260
00:23:38,341 --> 00:23:41,340
Rym: But anyway, things of the day.

261
00:23:42,741 --> 00:23:49,240
Rym: I don't even know, I just stumbled upon this, and it was funnier than I thought it would be, mostly because the writing's actually really good.

262
00:23:50,121 --> 00:24:10,320
Rym: This is a short Wario Land broadcast system public announcement, and it is basically a very short story presented as Wario giving a radio address to the citizens of Wario Land in response to the fact that Mario has invaded and fucked up his shit.

263
00:24:11,140 --> 00:24:15,760
Rym: And it's actually written to a degree that I found very funny.

264
00:24:16,100 --> 00:24:28,060
Scott: Well, I think the voice acting is good, and the plot is good, and the imagery is good, but the thing that bothers me is it's like, you know, Wario basically just flatly admits doing evil, like I said.

265
00:24:28,740 --> 00:24:38,820
Rym: That's what I thought was funny, the fact that he's like, "I admit that my regime was fully corrupt, and if any of the citizens of Wario Land wish to continue to support me anyway...".

266
00:24:39,040 --> 00:24:39,541
Rym: I always want

267
00:24:39,581 --> 00:24:51,720
Scott: villains to do that I would respect, so on the one hand, I like that, but on the other hand, I think it would have been funnier if he just said the lies that a real dictator would have said in that position to make it more real.

268
00:24:52,500 --> 00:25:07,399
Rym: I feel like both would be equally funny to me in different ways, like the inverse of like, Gaddafi just being like, "Here are all the war crimes I did, and lad, here's why I am now hiding in a bunker

269
00:25:07,439 --> 00:25:07,800
Rym: somewhere.".

270
00:25:08,502 --> 00:25:10,620
Scott: People don't even do that on their deathbeds for some reason.

271
00:25:11,060 --> 00:25:12,255
Rym: Nah, I feel like, I mean...

272
00:25:12,620 --> 00:25:13,258
Scott: What's up with that?

273
00:25:13,643 --> 00:25:15,020
Rym: On your deathbed, like... Let

274
00:25:15,061 --> 00:25:15,541
Scott: me tell

275
00:25:15,601 --> 00:25:15,761
Rym: you,

276
00:25:15,801 --> 00:25:29,339
Scott: if I did like a fucking, you know, obviously I wouldn't do a war crime, but if I did like a big art heist or a jewel thief or something, and then I was literally like going to die for sure, I'd be like, "I did the art

277
00:25:29,379 --> 00:25:29,680
Scott: heist.".

278
00:25:31,361 --> 00:25:34,539
Rym: So, funnily enough, in our Blades in the Dark game, and I guess I'm breaking...

279
00:25:34,661 --> 00:25:35,156
Scott: You didn't catch me!

280
00:25:35,742 --> 00:25:47,200
Rym: I'm breaking, we've been playing Blades in the Dark for like four years now in a long running campaign, but uh... So... I'm gonna... No character would ever reveal this, but we're the ones who killed Baz-O-Baz.

281
00:25:47,400 --> 00:25:51,200
Rym: We killed him in the first episode of this campaign, like four years ago.

282
00:25:51,741 --> 00:26:00,440
Rym: And the whole running theme of our campaign for four years has been that fucking nobody knows who killed Baz-O-Baz because we actually pulled it off and got away with it.

283
00:26:00,901 --> 00:26:08,340
Rym: And so far we've had like four different characters, even on their deathbeds, canonically refuse to reveal who killed Baz-O-Baz.

284
00:26:10,922 --> 00:26:17,420
Rym: The only things people reveal on their deathbeds is veterans who are senile tend to reveal that they saw UFOs.

285
00:26:17,700 --> 00:26:20,399
Rym: That seems to be the only thing that actually gets revealed on deathbeds.

286
00:26:20,740 --> 00:26:23,400
Rym: That or people who say they know who killed JFK.

287
00:26:25,908 --> 00:26:26,518
Scott: I know who did it.

288
00:26:28,947 --> 00:26:29,700
Scott: It's public knowledge.

289
00:26:29,700 --> 00:26:29,804
Scott: It is!

290
00:26:32,402 --> 00:26:34,220
Rym: There's a very simple answer to that question.

291
00:26:34,700 --> 00:26:36,800
Rym: It's almost as simple as who's in Grand Stome.

292
00:26:37,621 --> 00:26:38,576
Scott: I know, it's pretty much.

293
00:26:40,503 --> 00:26:41,920
Scott: Only the name's not in the question.

294
00:26:42,800 --> 00:26:43,620
Scott: So it's a little harder.

295
00:26:44,080 --> 00:26:45,000
Scott: You have to know some history.

296
00:26:45,782 --> 00:26:45,872
Rym: Yup.

297
00:26:46,281 --> 00:26:48,440
Rym: You gotta know one of those people who has three names in public.

298
00:26:49,364 --> 00:26:49,798
Scott: That's right.

299
00:26:50,401 --> 00:26:50,978
Rym: So what do you got?

300
00:26:52,403 --> 00:26:53,060
Scott: Oh, thing of the day.

301
00:26:53,200 --> 00:26:56,500
Scott: Okay, you know I love, you know, old games and fonts.

302
00:26:56,801 --> 00:27:04,480
Scott: So this person, what they did is this is a person who was also a nerd about old games and fonts.

303
00:27:05,860 --> 00:27:08,479
Scott: And there were some things that bothered them.

304
00:27:09,102 --> 00:27:11,300
Scott: And they went and investigated those things.

305
00:27:11,460 --> 00:27:18,460
Scott: And they're like, "Hey, I'm looking at this game Beyond Apple Manor from the PC and also this game Hard Hat Mac from the PC.

306
00:27:18,620 --> 00:27:22,279
Scott: And I'm looking at the fonts and those fonts are looking pretty similar.

307
00:27:22,960 --> 00:27:23,312
Scott: Similar

308
00:27:23,354 --> 00:27:23,519
Scott: fonts.".

309
00:27:23,580 --> 00:27:26,439
Scott: Because all those games, they weren't true type fonts then.

310
00:27:27,002 --> 00:27:28,620
Scott: It was all pixelated fonts, right?

311
00:27:28,880 --> 00:27:34,140
Scott: Someone drew each letter of the alphabet, like pixel by pixel, just like on the NES or whatever.

312
00:27:34,740 --> 00:27:36,079
Rym: Fonts were basically sprite sheets.

313
00:27:37,100 --> 00:27:37,720
Scott: Yeah, basically.

314
00:27:38,381 --> 00:27:41,860
Scott: And it's like, okay, these games are using similar fonts.

315
00:27:42,061 --> 00:27:46,340
Scott: So it wasn't like, you know, some game where someone made a font for that game.

316
00:27:46,441 --> 00:27:49,160
Scott: There was some font that came from somewhere else, right?

317
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:54,140
Scott: That these game developers got their font from somewhere and used it in their game.

318
00:27:55,621 --> 00:27:57,579
Scott: Where did they get those fonts from?

319
00:27:58,585 --> 00:28:00,140
Scott: Were the fonts slightly modified?

320
00:28:00,661 --> 00:28:04,140
Scott: What was the origins of these old game pixel fonts?

321
00:28:04,500 --> 00:28:07,198
Scott: Who really made them, et cetera, et cetera.

322
00:28:07,761 --> 00:28:11,700
Scott: And they go in deep through several games and examples.

323
00:28:11,960 --> 00:28:13,360
Rym: Oh, this is way more extensive than I thought.

324
00:28:13,480 --> 00:28:14,138
Rym: I just scrolled down.

325
00:28:14,380 --> 00:28:14,778
Rym: God damn.

326
00:28:15,783 --> 00:28:18,040
Scott: And the fonts are awesome.

327
00:28:18,161 --> 00:28:24,678
Scott: And also you'll learn about some games that you probably didn't even know existed, which I don't know if they're worth playing, but hey.

328
00:28:25,720 --> 00:28:27,058
Rym: Well, I don't know about any of these games.

329
00:28:28,440 --> 00:28:30,800
Scott: Yeah, you want to play some Raid Over Moscow?

330
00:28:31,080 --> 00:28:33,160
Rym: I feel like I want to watch a YouTube video of someone else playing it.

331
00:28:33,181 --> 00:28:34,000
Scott: I have an Apple 2.

332
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:35,700
Scott: We can play it on the Apple 2, for real.

333
00:28:36,220 --> 00:28:36,284
Scott: Ooh.

334
00:28:39,261 --> 00:28:43,580
Rym: In the meta moment, the GeekNights Book Club book is The Night is Short.

335
00:28:44,141 --> 00:28:45,019
Rym: Walk on, girl.

336
00:28:45,463 --> 00:28:48,200
Rym: I have paused reading it until Scott resumes reading it.

337
00:28:49,128 --> 00:28:49,360
Scott: Okay.

338
00:28:50,024 --> 00:28:50,919
Scott: Maybe I'll do that soon.

339
00:28:51,928 --> 00:28:52,379
Rym: It's a good book.

340
00:28:52,500 --> 00:28:53,460
Rym: We'll probably do the episode.

341
00:28:53,721 --> 00:28:55,360
Rym: I would guess in March we'll do the episode.

342
00:28:55,360 --> 00:28:56,407
Rym: It's not a long book.

343
00:28:56,507 --> 00:28:58,379
Rym: once we actually read it, but we'll give you time to read it.

344
00:28:59,261 --> 00:29:10,120
Rym: If you didn't listen to our episode on the movie The Night is Short, Walk on, girl, and you've never seen the movie The Night is Short, Walk on, girl, I'd suggest either reading this book or watching the movie because I like them both a lot.

345
00:29:12,841 --> 00:29:20,800
Rym: We will be live at PAX East 2024 with not one, but two panels, but they're both on Sunday.

346
00:29:22,581 --> 00:29:24,840
Rym: So every game is the same game.

347
00:29:25,260 --> 00:29:28,320
Rym: Our all new panel will be Sunday at 2.30 in the Dragonfly Theater.

348
00:29:29,021 --> 00:29:35,460
Rym: And then a panel that is a rehash of an old format, but with entirely new content.

349
00:29:36,001 --> 00:29:40,100
Rym: We've done many versions of the end tabletop games you must play.

350
00:29:40,682 --> 00:29:46,940
Rym: But only once did we did a panel that was just the end games you must play, tabletop or not.

351
00:29:47,941 --> 00:29:52,540
Scott: Now I got to say, shoving us on Sunday because we're just some guys.

352
00:29:52,840 --> 00:29:53,600
Scott: We're not industry.

353
00:29:53,600 --> 00:29:54,540
Scott: We're not famous.

354
00:29:54,781 --> 00:29:55,180
Scott: We're not anything.

355
00:29:55,300 --> 00:29:56,199
Scott: It's like that makes sense.

356
00:29:57,103 --> 00:30:00,220
Scott: That's fine even though we'll get less crowd on Sunday.

357
00:30:01,380 --> 00:30:01,894
Scott: But you know what?

358
00:30:03,282 --> 00:30:07,733
Scott: That's actually kind of really convenient because that means I only got to carry my shit over to the con once.

359
00:30:08,584 --> 00:30:10,160
Rym: We do a panel at 2.30.

360
00:30:10,160 --> 00:30:11,160
Rym: At 3.30 we're done.

361
00:30:11,640 --> 00:30:12,719
Rym: We chill for a little bit.

362
00:30:12,981 --> 00:30:14,760
Rym: We do another panel at 4.30.

363
00:30:14,760 --> 00:30:17,820
Rym: We'll probably miss or walk into the end of the Omega-Thon, but I can live with that.

364
00:30:17,920 --> 00:30:18,400
Rym: And then we're free.

365
00:30:18,801 --> 00:30:20,340
Scott: Oh, has anyone even come to that panel?

366
00:30:20,380 --> 00:30:20,718
Scott: Oh no.

367
00:30:21,201 --> 00:30:23,700
Rym: I mean the Omega-Thon doesn't get a ton of attendance lately.

368
00:30:24,160 --> 00:30:24,618
Scott: That's true.

369
00:30:25,180 --> 00:30:31,740
Rym: But also panel attendance at PAX has been way up since the dark times have wound down.

370
00:30:32,480 --> 00:30:37,160
Rym: And a lot of people, I still think this PAX East is going to be as off the hook as Unplugged was.

371
00:30:37,280 --> 00:30:38,380
Scott: Maybe I'll be wrong.

372
00:30:38,400 --> 00:30:39,260
Scott: Oh yeah, it's going to be crowded.

373
00:30:39,400 --> 00:30:47,820
Rym: But if it is, like Unplugged and even MagFest, even panels we had at Squirrely Times, like a lot of people came relative to how many people I expected to come.

374
00:30:48,200 --> 00:30:59,220
Rym: It felt like PAX is from like the 2012s in terms of the number of people who would come despite weird time, weird title, like our kind of panel content.

375
00:30:59,881 --> 00:31:00,719
Rym: So we'll see.

376
00:31:01,020 --> 00:31:01,717
Rym: Should be a good time.

377
00:31:02,362 --> 00:31:03,960
Scott: Otherwise PAX East is going to be great.

378
00:31:04,160 --> 00:31:04,759
Rym: We're going to be there.

379
00:31:04,800 --> 00:31:07,799
Rym: And if you didn't get a badge, I don't know if it's going to sell out.

380
00:31:08,280 --> 00:31:09,297
Scott: I actually haven't sold out yet.

381
00:31:10,922 --> 00:31:13,119
Rym: PAX East is the biggest capacity PAX.

382
00:31:13,600 --> 00:31:15,159
Rym: The only limit really is the hotels.

383
00:31:18,045 --> 00:31:19,640
Scott: I did acquire the Geek Night stickers.

384
00:31:19,920 --> 00:31:22,420
Scott: If you're watching us on YouTube, you might be, you can see one.

385
00:31:23,042 --> 00:31:25,020
Scott: It's our logo only shiny on a sticker.

386
00:31:25,524 --> 00:31:26,520
Scott: It's kind of actually big.

387
00:31:26,680 --> 00:31:27,178
Scott: It's like, you know.

388
00:31:27,703 --> 00:31:28,019
Scott: I like it.

389
00:31:28,421 --> 00:31:29,660
Rym: So now one last time.

390
00:31:29,982 --> 00:31:30,659
Scott: I made a pile of them.

391
00:31:30,740 --> 00:31:33,439
Scott: They cost like almost a dollar each maybe.

392
00:31:34,802 --> 00:31:37,740
Rym: I mean our business cards cost a dollar each and I feel like it's worth it.

393
00:31:38,741 --> 00:31:40,354
Rym: And they're pretty high quality stickers.

394
00:31:41,486 --> 00:31:42,119
Scott: They're not chintzy.

395
00:31:42,400 --> 00:31:47,336
Rym: Remember that one time when Jason Rohr was giving out just dollar bills with info stamped on them?

396
00:31:48,302 --> 00:31:48,390
Scott: Yeah.

397
00:31:50,361 --> 00:31:52,760
Rym: But one last time, a warning for all of you out there.

398
00:31:53,441 --> 00:32:00,100
Rym: I don't care what you do with the Geek Night sticker if you get a hold of one outside of a convention that we attend.

399
00:32:00,240 --> 00:32:12,459
Rym: Do not fucking stick them to anything within the bounds of a convention that we attend because I don't want to have to explain to like PAX why there's a Geek Night sticker on something expensive.

400
00:32:14,122 --> 00:32:17,460
Scott: On something that doesn't belong to the person who placed the sticker on it.

401
00:32:17,680 --> 00:32:23,240
Rym: And if a sticker causes such a situation, I will try to track you down myself.

402
00:32:23,481 --> 00:32:26,599
Rym: Like I'll try, I'll tell them everyone I gave a fucking sticker to.

403
00:32:27,581 --> 00:32:32,960
Scott: And then I will punish you with adhesive because that's appropriate to the crime.

404
00:32:34,822 --> 00:32:38,380
Rym: We recently in our Blades in the Dark punished someone with an adhesive and it was fantastic.

405
00:32:40,042 --> 00:32:43,280
Scott: If you have a lot of hair, you might not enjoy such a punishment.

406
00:32:46,480 --> 00:32:47,967
Rym: Alright, so anti-cheat.

407
00:32:48,028 --> 00:32:49,254
Rym: because we just talked about how.

408
00:32:49,354 --> 00:32:54,779
Rym: one of the reasons why I'm not willing to play Helldivers 2 right now is that there's onerous anti-cheat in a game that doesn't need it.

409
00:32:54,920 --> 00:32:57,449
Scott: Yep, we've talked about cheating a lot.

410
00:32:57,650 --> 00:33:02,039
Scott: We haven't actually talked about how to cheat or how to get away with cheating, have we?

411
00:33:03,280 --> 00:33:07,220
Scott: We've talked about what is cheating and things of that nature.

412
00:33:08,463 --> 00:33:10,560
Scott: But yeah, how do you actually prevent cheating?

413
00:33:10,800 --> 00:33:26,100
Scott: If you are in charge of making, designing a game, or administering a game, or refereeing a game of some kind, what techniques and methods are effective in preventing cheating?

414
00:33:26,400 --> 00:33:37,516
Scott: Because while all of us here won't actually cheat, there are other people in the world who are of ill moral repute and they will cheat.

415
00:33:37,937 --> 00:33:38,599
Scott: They will do this.

416
00:33:39,582 --> 00:33:43,480
Scott: And if they are playing your game, they are going to cheat at your game.

417
00:33:43,580 --> 00:33:46,520
Scott: How can you detect them and stop them?

418
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:54,045
Rym: And I want to point out, it takes the exact same ingenuity to figure out how to catch a cheater as it does to cheat.

419
00:33:54,406 --> 00:33:59,619
Rym: So people who are good at one are good at the other whether or not they use that power for good or evil.

420
00:34:00,400 --> 00:34:17,679
Scott: Yep, now the very first thing is that regardless of the type of cheating and the type of game and the type of anything, in order to prevent or take any measure to stop cheating, the first thing you must always have is some kind of authority and power.

421
00:34:18,340 --> 00:34:23,795
Scott: If you do not have authority and power of some kind within the game's realm, then you are hopeless.

422
00:34:25,321 --> 00:34:29,274
Scott: There's nothing that you can do to prevent or stop cheating.

423
00:34:31,719 --> 00:34:40,620
Scott: Now that power doesn't necessarily mean that you have to be like the judge at the Magic Tournament who actually has some power to disqualify someone.

424
00:34:40,659 --> 00:34:43,620
Rym: But that power exists and is vested somewhere.

425
00:34:44,560 --> 00:34:44,741
Scott: Right.

426
00:34:45,924 --> 00:35:00,859
Scott: But that power simply might be, you called someone out, you have evidence, and the other people, you know, there are enough other people there who agree that that is not cool and are willing to rationally examine your evidence, etc.

427
00:35:01,442 --> 00:35:10,480
Scott: It's like, you know, if you're just at a table with four friends, you're all equals playing the same game, and, you know, you might see that someone's just fucking cheating.

428
00:35:10,881 --> 00:35:13,720
Scott: Your fucking uncle, he's just cheating over there, right?

429
00:35:14,021 --> 00:35:24,506
Rym: We had this happen, remember that party years ago with a certain group of people, and they were playing one of those poker games where like one of your cards is visible on your forehead, and then we played another game with them.

430
00:35:24,526 --> 00:35:27,720
Rym: that was some trivia game, it wasn't Pictionary, it was one of those games.

431
00:35:28,120 --> 00:35:38,480
Rym: And in both cases, people in this social group openly cheated, and when Scott and I called them out, they got mad and ended the game in both times at the same party.

432
00:35:39,642 --> 00:36:01,920
Scott: Yeah, I don't remember this, but regardless, the point is, if you're at a group, and, you know, let's say you're playing a game with your family and your uncle's cheating, and if you call him out, right, you detected it, you saw it, you maybe even collected evidence, like recorded it on your phone, and such and such, right, you're all on equal footing, the only way that you could actually take action is if the other people at the table give a shit.

433
00:36:02,481 --> 00:36:15,840
Scott: If the other people, if your cousins and your mom and your grandpa, if they don't care, then uncle's just gonna get away with cheating, 'cause there's no power involved or authority to do anything about it.

434
00:36:15,840 --> 00:36:22,178
Rym: I mean, if I cross-check a dude in ice hockey as hard as I can, and the ref doesn't blow the whistle, I totally got away with it.

435
00:36:23,040 --> 00:36:26,380
Scott: Right, and then it would be up to, that's why there's hockey fights, right?

436
00:36:26,961 --> 00:36:29,300
Scott: Hockey fights would be the enforcement method.

437
00:36:29,400 --> 00:36:34,700
Rym: I mean, family games where someone cheats have definitely historically turned into fights.

438
00:36:35,820 --> 00:36:47,200
Scott: Sure enough, right, but the point is, there needs to be some sort of power and authority vested somewhere that you can access to stop the cheating, otherwise nothing else matters.

439
00:36:47,420 --> 00:36:52,880
Rym: Or in the very least, to mete out consequences, however small those consequences might be.

440
00:36:53,140 --> 00:36:57,780
Scott: It could simply be ending the game, not playing with that person anymore.

441
00:36:58,041 --> 00:37:03,159
Scott: You know, correcting the game and fixing the cheat that they did.

442
00:37:03,980 --> 00:37:07,640
Scott: Or, who knows, admonishing them and continuing, right?

443
00:37:08,321 --> 00:37:09,220
Scott: Something, right?

444
00:37:10,083 --> 00:37:14,040
Scott: You can't do any of those things without some amount of power and authority.

445
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:23,420
Rym: I want to start with a historical example that's also a personal anecdote of cheating in this context, because I feel like examples are a good way to explore anti-cheat.

446
00:37:23,862 --> 00:37:25,128
Rym: So, the game of Euchre.

447
00:37:25,832 --> 00:37:27,540
Rym: We did this in Hearts 2, but we'll just use Euchre.

448
00:37:27,720 --> 00:37:43,420
Rym: It's a simple trick-taking game that people in Michigan play and nobody else has either heard of or cares about, and it's not a great game, I'm not getting into that, but in this game, like in many card games, it is very easy for someone who is skilled at sleight of hand to just cheat.

449
00:37:44,501 --> 00:37:48,880
Rym: Just like, think about an old-timey Western poker game for high-stakes money.

450
00:37:49,583 --> 00:37:53,940
Rym: It is very easy for someone with sleight of hand in poker to cheat in some capacity.

451
00:37:54,660 --> 00:38:02,134
Scott: Yeah, all of these, you know, people who are like, you know, magicians who practice sleight of hand and do all kinds of, like, those people could cheat at cards instead.

452
00:38:02,154 --> 00:38:02,898
Scott: They don't.

453
00:38:03,621 --> 00:38:05,639
Scott: 'Cause magic is a much safer career.

454
00:38:06,921 --> 00:38:11,080
Scott: And if they're good at it, they can make plenty of money doing that, and it's more fun, probably.

455
00:38:11,540 --> 00:38:15,220
Rym: So, how did old-timey Western poker games prevent cheating?

456
00:38:16,660 --> 00:38:28,320
Rym: Yeah, the fact that there really weren't cops or laws for a brief period of time in the Old West, and dipshits with guns would just settle their own problems in horrific ways.

457
00:38:29,342 --> 00:38:34,600
Scott: We didn't pull guns out in Michigan, but-- I'm sure people have been shot for cheating who didn't cheat.

458
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:35,910
Rym: Oh, yeah, absolutely.

459
00:38:36,152 --> 00:38:40,359
Scott: In fact, I am-- Someone you don't like, just play poker with someone you don't like, accuse them of cheating and shoot 'em.

460
00:38:41,160 --> 00:38:44,060
Rym: Yeah, a perfectly valid strategy if you have no morals.

461
00:38:44,502 --> 00:38:53,340
Rym: So, talking about Euchre, you know, in a modern setting, as a kid, it's very easy to cheat at a game like Euchre, especially because you'd use half a deck of cards.

462
00:38:53,380 --> 00:39:01,700
Rym: Like, it's a small number of cards, you deal five cards to each player, the substance of those cards matter, and you're not allowed to talk to your partner.

463
00:39:01,780 --> 00:39:04,640
Rym: So there's lots of mechanisms one could cheat.

464
00:39:05,280 --> 00:39:12,160
Rym: So the anti-cheat that has been implemented pretty universally in games like Euchre involved things like cutting the deck.

465
00:39:13,082 --> 00:39:14,680
Rym: People don't even do this that much anymore.

466
00:39:14,880 --> 00:39:20,980
Rym: I've found very few people at tables actually want to cut a deck the way most card games used to prescribe.

467
00:39:21,380 --> 00:39:23,180
Rym: It seems like it's a cultural tradition that's disappearing.

468
00:39:23,760 --> 00:39:27,439
Scott: The concept that whoever shuffles-- I always cut the deck when I'm playing real card games.

469
00:39:27,560 --> 00:39:37,280
Scott: Oh, yeah, if I'm playing a dame for real, I always want to offer that, but I've found-- When you played Netrunner, you'd shuffle your deck, hand it to the other person, and then they would shuffle it and hand it back, and then you couldn't shuffle it anymore.

470
00:39:37,983 --> 00:39:41,580
Rym: So if I was the dealer, I shuffle the deck, and I may have cheated.

471
00:39:41,840 --> 00:39:45,740
Rym: I may have lined up the cards in any particular matter I want, because it's really fucking easy to do that.

472
00:39:46,140 --> 00:40:06,839
Rym: But then I had to hand the deck to another player who is opposed to me, and they can't fuss with the deck, but they pick a number of arbitrary cards from the top and set them aside, and I have to take the bottom of that and stick it on top and then deal out the cards, thus ensuring that I don't have full agency over the order of those cards in case I happen to have sleight of hand.

473
00:40:07,500 --> 00:40:20,820
Rym: Now, of course, I was still able to cheat because there are mechanisms like the false cut or dealing from the bottom that are very hard to detect, and those are really only detectable by everyone is watching the dealer.

474
00:40:21,280 --> 00:40:31,680
Rym: That's actually why Euchre requires you to deal 2-3, 2-3, 2-3, because the ritual of that makes it harder to slip a card out from the bottom without someone noticing because so few cards are--.

475
00:40:31,700 --> 00:40:33,130
Scott: Well, on the twos, it's really easy.

476
00:40:33,150 --> 00:40:34,660
Scott: You just take one for the top and one for the bottom.

477
00:40:35,021 --> 00:40:37,068
Rym: Well, usually you go one, two.

478
00:40:37,490 --> 00:40:50,099
Rym: The top and bottom simultaneously, I do that in spotted, but if anyone ever did one motion to deal out two cards, I immediately called bullshit and demanded a redeal, as any savvy player would do.

479
00:40:50,940 --> 00:40:59,040
Rym: But we had this physical mechanism to prevent the most common form of cheating, and it was pretty effective, but again, it required everything Scott said.

480
00:40:59,320 --> 00:41:09,460
Rym: The people at the table had to agree to that and follow it, but the game encoded the rules, the culture encoded the rules, and it was an effective anti-cheat against all but the most extreme magician.

481
00:41:11,063 --> 00:41:21,320
Scott: So this type of cheating, right, because there's different-- I think there's really not that actually many categories of cheating, but this category of cheating is basically not following game procedures, right?

482
00:41:21,480 --> 00:41:35,040
Scott: Games have procedures that you're supposed to follow that are-- have, you know, based on how well the rules are written, have different degrees of explicitness and ambiguity, and if those procedures aren't followed properly, then that's cheating, right?

483
00:41:35,100 --> 00:41:38,880
Rym: Yep, and the rules of a card game, the deck must be shuffled and delivered randomly.

484
00:41:39,920 --> 00:41:45,920
Scott: Yep, and so the procedures themselves are often anti-cheat mechanisms, right?

485
00:41:46,000 --> 00:41:55,580
Scott: It's like if the procedures are followed, and that actually prevents other problems in the game, you know, it makes it harder to cheat in other ways as long as we do these things.

486
00:41:55,820 --> 00:41:57,040
Rym: Case in point, American football.

487
00:41:57,040 --> 00:42:00,759
Scott: It's an anti-cheat mechanism, but also not following them is a form of cheating itself.

488
00:42:01,301 --> 00:42:15,440
Rym: The fact that in American football, the coin flip, the coin is flipped into the air, but it is not caught, it is left to land on the ground, because a flipped and caught coin is very easy to cheat with, and I actually learned how to do this--.

489
00:42:15,440 --> 00:42:25,658
Scott: Also, they use those weird commemorative coins that I believe are 50/50, so-- Never ever-- If you got-- you could get info about the coin in advance, you could predict which side is more likely to land up and--

490
00:42:26,481 --> 00:42:40,040
Rym: But never ever trust, for anything serious, a coin flip where the person flipping catches it, because I learned in RIT, and I can do it pretty reliably, you can make a flipped coin that you catch be whatever face you want.

491
00:42:41,403 --> 00:42:43,859
Rym: Because they spin at a rate that you can predict.

492
00:42:47,741 --> 00:42:56,200
Scott: Anyway, so the way to prevent this kind of cheating, right, is to simply have people pay the fuck attention, right?

493
00:42:56,820 --> 00:42:59,320
Scott: That's all you gotta do to prevent this kind of cheating, right?

494
00:42:59,600 --> 00:43:13,560
Scott: Obviously you need the authority and whatnot, but it's like, if you just pay attention to the procedures, and actually look closely, and follow them, and don't drift off or whatever, it's like, then cheating will be prevented.

495
00:43:13,780 --> 00:43:15,279
Scott: It's really not complicated, right?

496
00:43:15,480 --> 00:43:23,279
Rym: It's like-- No one got away with cheating except the pros at our Euchre tables, because everyone was watching the dealer with an eagle eye.

497
00:43:26,684 --> 00:43:41,340
Scott: As long as things are done openly, where everyone can see them, and people are paying attention because they care, then it would be pretty difficult for somebody to execute the procedures of the game in a way that's shady, right?

498
00:43:41,420 --> 00:43:45,395
Scott: It's like, someone tries to start a game monopoly and deal themselves an extra $500 bill, right?

499
00:43:47,282 --> 00:43:53,400
Scott: It's like, if you just look at everyone's money, you'd be like, "Hey, why you got an extra $500 more than everyone else?

500
00:43:53,460 --> 00:43:54,489
Scott: You're supposed to start with $1100, not

501
00:43:54,489 --> 00:43:55,760
Scott: $1600.".

502
00:43:55,760 --> 00:43:55,848
Scott: Yeah.

503
00:43:56,780 --> 00:43:56,868
Scott: Yeah.

504
00:43:57,521 --> 00:43:59,359
Scott: It's like, it's pretty much that simple.

505
00:43:59,921 --> 00:44:01,960
Scott: It's like, just fucking count.

506
00:44:03,420 --> 00:44:11,640
Rym: But then there's the really thorny space that especially applies to video games of, how did you frame it, modifying the equipment in some way.

507
00:44:11,701 --> 00:44:13,880
Scott: Right, so this is the hardest kind of cheating.

508
00:44:14,020 --> 00:44:23,440
Scott: It's like, games, especially sports, and also esports included, are games that involve specialized equipment, right?

509
00:44:23,720 --> 00:44:29,260
Scott: I think you'd also, even if you think about anything that's a sport, whether it's baseball, corked bats, right?

510
00:44:30,761 --> 00:44:33,800
Scott: Running, which is drugged up human bodies, right?

511
00:44:34,100 --> 00:44:36,100
Scott: The equipment is your body, which has been modified.

512
00:44:36,680 --> 00:44:40,758
Scott: Jenga, someone shaves some of the Jenga tiles like this, logs, right?

513
00:44:41,744 --> 00:44:46,259
Rym: Ice hockey, someone's got to stick with a little too much curve or a goalie pad that's a little too big.

514
00:44:46,841 --> 00:44:50,120
Scott: Playing cards, someone marks them or clips them, right?

515
00:44:50,620 --> 00:44:54,620
Rym: Counter-Strike, you've got an aimbot running on your computer next to Counter-Strike.

516
00:44:54,620 --> 00:44:57,000
Scott: Right, the software is part of the equipment, right?

517
00:44:58,263 --> 00:45:00,498
Scott: You've got a robot that moves the mouse for you.

518
00:45:02,481 --> 00:45:11,140
Scott: There's equipment and materials involved in games, and if those materials aren't according to a particular spec, then that's cheating.

519
00:45:11,943 --> 00:45:22,940
Scott: The worst case would be something like Formula One, where it's like the car must comply with these exact measurements, weights, et cetera, materials, you know, costs and shapes and all kinds of rules.

520
00:45:23,762 --> 00:45:30,515
Scott: And the way you stop this kind of cheating is also pretty simple, which is inspect the equipment closely, right?

521
00:45:32,380 --> 00:45:40,720
Scott: The difficulty, of course, is that sometimes you can't really inspect the equipment or be a super-certain, and this is where you get the performance-enhancing drugs, right?

522
00:45:40,780 --> 00:45:43,796
Scott: It's like you can't always be 100% sure.

523
00:45:44,620 --> 00:45:50,377
Scott: We have tests, we drug-tested someone, they got caught, and then they say, "Oh, no, my...".

524
00:45:51,021 --> 00:45:53,838
Scott: The reason a Russian figure skater, her excuse was that...

525
00:45:54,100 --> 00:45:55,220
Rym: What, the strawberry or something?

526
00:45:55,660 --> 00:46:13,500
Scott: Yeah, so the excuse, if you haven't heard the excuse of the Russian figure skater, is that her grandpa would make this strawberry dessert, like family meal thing, and grandpa smashed his pills on a cutting board and then made the strawberry dessert on the cutting board later.

527
00:46:14,300 --> 00:46:19,520
Scott: And so somehow some minuscule amount of whatever got into the strawberry.

528
00:46:19,780 --> 00:46:23,920
Rym: I was walking down the street and I just fell on a needle filled with steroids.

529
00:46:24,500 --> 00:46:29,638
Scott: And that was somehow enough for the drug test to come up positive, whatever minuscule amount that might have been.

530
00:46:31,425 --> 00:46:33,060
Scott: It's like, yeah, obviously that's bullshit.

531
00:46:35,286 --> 00:46:39,440
Scott: But even at the same time, something like that could happen.

532
00:46:40,523 --> 00:46:57,144
Scott: If I was in the Olympic Village and I know that I'm the second-fastest runner and I'm chilling out with the first-fastest runner and he leaves his smoothie on the table and I sprinkle some drug, some performance-enhancing drug powder in his smoothie, I'm chilling

533
00:46:57,305 --> 00:47:05,420
Rym: out in the Overwatch Village before a tournament and I see that one of my opponents has left their computer unlocked.

534
00:47:05,500 --> 00:47:08,598
Rym: I go install some cheating software on it.

535
00:47:09,400 --> 00:47:10,247
Scott: Right now that is cheating.

536
00:47:10,650 --> 00:47:11,860
Scott: You're the one who's cheating, not them.

537
00:47:12,384 --> 00:47:13,600
Scott: And they're the one who gets caught for cheating.

538
00:47:13,640 --> 00:47:16,960
Rym: I mean, if you spike someone else's drink with steroids, you're cheating.

539
00:47:17,502 --> 00:47:18,420
Scott: Well, that's also a crime.

540
00:47:19,871 --> 00:47:32,900
Rym: But this comes down to the onerousness of imposing these checks and rules coupled with the possibility of consequences and coupled with the stakes involved.

541
00:47:33,300 --> 00:47:50,200
Rym: So Counter-Strike or a video game like Helldiver, all these video game situations, anti-cheat software has to walk a fine line between preventing someone from using software they shouldn't to get an unfair advantage in the game and breaking your computer or spying on you or basically itself being malware.

542
00:47:51,280 --> 00:47:55,780
Scott: Right, you have to look at how serious is the game that we're playing, right?

543
00:47:55,920 --> 00:47:57,086
Scott: Is this the World Series?

544
00:47:57,127 --> 00:48:01,160
Scott: Yeah, we're fucking x-raying every goddamn baseball bat that enters this building, right?

545
00:48:02,142 --> 00:48:05,860
Rym: I'd let him use the cork bats, though, because the cork bats every study shows aren't better.

546
00:48:06,242 --> 00:48:07,339
Rym: In fact, they're probably worse.

547
00:48:08,081 --> 00:48:11,020
Scott: I'm just saying it's against the rules whether it's better or not, right?

548
00:48:11,080 --> 00:48:14,920
Scott: So therefore, you're cheating if you try it, regardless of whether it's actually going to help you.

549
00:48:15,185 --> 00:48:15,879
Scott: It's against the rules.

550
00:48:16,461 --> 00:48:17,199
Scott: You know, it's the Olympics.

551
00:48:17,422 --> 00:48:22,380
Scott: Yeah, everyone's going to live in this village where everything is closely guarded and you can't sneak anything in, hopefully, right?

552
00:48:22,461 --> 00:48:23,296
Scott: Even though you probably can't.

553
00:48:24,402 --> 00:48:26,760
Scott: Is it Poker Night at Rym's house?

554
00:48:27,040 --> 00:48:27,698
Scott: Yeah, guess what?

555
00:48:28,503 --> 00:48:30,359
Scott: If you use marked cards, you'll probably get away with it.

556
00:48:31,021 --> 00:48:33,240
Scott: Because nobody's looking that close.

557
00:48:33,640 --> 00:48:34,826
Rym: The $12 you make is not worth anyone's time.

558
00:48:38,304 --> 00:48:39,240
Scott: Is it a poker tournament?

559
00:48:39,320 --> 00:48:49,979
Scott: Yeah, those decks are all coming from a vault where they've been all checked and printed especially, and no one's ever seen them before, and we're going to change the deck every game.

560
00:48:50,761 --> 00:48:53,520
Rym: In that case, cheating usually would come down to corruption.

561
00:48:55,027 --> 00:48:55,380
Scott: Indeed.

562
00:48:55,840 --> 00:49:00,759
Rym: But corruption is really just an evolved form of violating the procedures of the game.

563
00:49:01,921 --> 00:49:07,800
Rym: Like, the referee is supposed to call X and you bribe them to not notice X at a very particular moment.

564
00:49:08,362 --> 00:49:20,960
Scott: The difference between corruption and simply procedures not being followed properly is that in corruption, this is where the people who have the power and authority to stop the cheating are in fact part of the cheating.

565
00:49:21,200 --> 00:49:22,280
Scott: And that's what's different, right?

566
00:49:22,721 --> 00:49:29,880
Scott: If the hockey ref wants your team to win and he just calls them bullshit penalties a couple times, right?

567
00:49:30,682 --> 00:49:35,860
Scott: It's like, you know, yes, they're not following the correct procedure.

568
00:49:35,940 --> 00:49:39,200
Scott: They called a penalty inappropriately when no penalty was warranted.

569
00:49:39,801 --> 00:49:47,120
Scott: So that is true, but the authority to do anything about it is vested in the cheating party.

570
00:49:47,444 --> 00:49:47,516
Scott: Yup.

571
00:49:48,181 --> 00:49:49,659
Scott: So what the fuck are you going to do about that?

572
00:49:49,840 --> 00:49:53,159
Rym: That's why the Black Sox are known as the Black Sox to this day.

573
00:49:54,020 --> 00:49:54,377
Scott: That's right.

574
00:49:55,341 --> 00:49:57,393
Scott: But that was actually, the refs weren't in on that.

575
00:49:58,620 --> 00:49:59,480
Scott: The umps weren't in on that.

576
00:49:59,520 --> 00:50:03,299
Scott: That was the players were losing on purpose and betting on themselves to lose.

577
00:50:04,525 --> 00:50:11,499
Rym: Well then it's the question of, are you cheating at game A or is game A's cheating just a part of a larger cheating of game B?

578
00:50:11,780 --> 00:50:13,339
Rym: Game B being society in some way.

579
00:50:14,740 --> 00:50:27,119
Rym: But for what we just said before, the analogy of corruption in a video game would be what if the anti-cheat in Overwatch had a "if Rym is playing, do not enforce" because Rym is part of the fix.

580
00:50:28,420 --> 00:50:31,720
Scott: Yeah, Rym works at the game company and he puts in code to help him.

581
00:50:31,861 --> 00:50:33,190
Scott: This has happened before.

582
00:50:33,371 --> 00:50:34,580
Scott: I can't remember the exact instance.

583
00:50:35,481 --> 00:50:44,480
Scott: But it has happened before where people who worked at game companies gave themselves boosts in the games for their own favor.

584
00:50:45,140 --> 00:50:53,500
Rym: Now one interesting sort of side specific to video games is I think there's the situation where the game has high stakes and matters.

585
00:50:53,760 --> 00:50:59,060
Rym: Like the professional championship of a game where that requires this high degree of anti-cheat.

586
00:50:59,702 --> 00:51:06,160
Rym: But there's also a separate factor of a game that people can just play, the masses can play, like Counter-Strike.

587
00:51:07,241 --> 00:51:28,903
Rym: In the era when Counter-Strike became popular, but before there was effective anti-cheat mechanisms the presence of cheating, it wasn't high stakes, the stakes of Counter-Strike are basically negative just playing it against randos, but it literally ruined the game and made it unplayable at all if anyone cheated.

588
00:51:30,187 --> 00:51:34,820
Rym: In which case, anti-cheat is required for the game to exist in the first place.

589
00:51:35,600 --> 00:51:42,479
Rym: And because people wanted to play Counter-Strike, that's kind of why people were willing to put up with Punkbuster and VAC in those early days.

590
00:51:43,440 --> 00:51:44,042
Scott: That's correct.

591
00:51:45,749 --> 00:51:53,580
Scott: So the last category of cheating that I can think of at least is basically there's an illegal game state that is created.

592
00:51:53,660 --> 00:52:04,037
Scott: Now you can sort of say that maybe procedures weren't followed correctly, but it could just be like "Hey, somebody did something they're not allowed to

593
00:52:04,077 --> 00:52:04,218
Scott: do.".

594
00:52:05,663 --> 00:52:21,138
Scott: Maybe you're playing a collectible card game and there's a card that says "You can only have two greens" and someone puts out three greens and forgets that there's a rule for two greens and no one catches it.

595
00:52:22,162 --> 00:52:24,170
Scott: And it's like, they might have done that on purpose.

596
00:52:24,913 --> 00:52:26,460
Scott: Just slipped a third green out there.

597
00:52:27,581 --> 00:52:32,440
Scott: I guess that technically, it's a matter of semantics, was that not following a procedure correctly.

598
00:52:33,061 --> 00:52:40,820
Scott: But it's sort of different from not following a procedure correctly in that it's not just like you didn't shuffle the cards or you didn't cut them.

599
00:52:40,920 --> 00:52:46,158
Scott: It's like the game is actually in a state in which the rules are broken.

600
00:52:48,860 --> 00:52:52,499
Rym: Two pucks appear on the ice and we're not sure which one was the official puck.

601
00:52:53,440 --> 00:52:53,541
Scott: Right.

602
00:52:53,561 --> 00:52:56,117
Scott: They have a rule, which is you blow the whistle, you stop play, etc.

603
00:52:57,040 --> 00:53:00,000
Scott: of what to do in that situation so it doesn't completely break the game.

604
00:53:00,581 --> 00:53:04,420
Scott: But it's like you still had, for a time, an illegal game state.

605
00:53:04,480 --> 00:53:09,520
Rym: And this is where you need a mechanism to detect, prevent, and mitigate those situations.

606
00:53:09,982 --> 00:53:24,640
Rym: And I think you need a separate mechanism on at what point do you determine intentionality and/or apply consequences beyond the consequences of making the game into a good state or ending the game because it's permanently ruined.

607
00:53:26,220 --> 00:53:28,739
Rym: Hockey, like a good example, just to keep bringing hockey analogies.

608
00:53:30,101 --> 00:53:36,740
Rym: Delay of game, if you're in your zone and you shoot the puck just out of the arena, like off into the crowd, that's a delay of game penalty.

609
00:53:37,181 --> 00:53:39,200
Rym: It doesn't matter if you did it on purpose or not.

610
00:53:39,300 --> 00:53:51,315
Rym: The fact that it happened is a penalty that interacts with the game because otherwise people would very clearly, "Oops, I accidentally knocked it out of play in spicy

611
00:53:51,355 --> 00:53:52,018
Rym: situations.".

612
00:53:52,500 --> 00:53:55,356
Rym: "Oops, I accidentally had four Sol Rings in my magic

613
00:53:55,397 --> 00:53:55,618
Rym: deck.".

614
00:53:56,060 --> 00:54:01,518
Rym: "I guess my dad left them on the cutting board and I must have accidentally shuffled them in right before the

615
00:54:01,538 --> 00:54:01,919
Rym: tournament.".

616
00:54:02,981 --> 00:54:03,064
Scott: Yep.

617
00:54:05,781 --> 00:54:10,014
Scott: So yeah, to prevent illegal game states, the best thing you can do is to have a video game, right?

618
00:54:12,541 --> 00:54:19,680
Scott: Or some other sort of system that monitors the game state and that system is not corrupted.

619
00:54:20,821 --> 00:54:26,940
Scott: And it monitors the game state and notifies or does something when the game state is no good, right?

620
00:54:27,040 --> 00:54:36,216
Scott: So if you have a video game and there's a PVP video game and there's a server and the server obviously doesn't have cheat installed on it because it's at the game company's data

621
00:54:36,236 --> 00:54:36,437
Rym: center,

622
00:54:38,981 --> 00:54:51,380
Scott: it can detect, hopefully, it might not be able to detect if someone's cheating, depending on the type of cheating, but it can detect like, "Hey, so-and-so has more ammo than that gun can possibly hold," right?

623
00:54:51,861 --> 00:54:55,899
Scott: "Hey, so-and-so has a thousand health in a game where max health is a

624
00:54:55,959 --> 00:54:56,180
Scott: hundred.".

625
00:54:56,260 --> 00:55:00,916
Rym: A really common one in the Quake days was, "So-and-so's character reached a speed that should be

626
00:55:00,956 --> 00:55:01,498
Rym: impossible.".

627
00:55:02,242 --> 00:55:02,805
Scott: Exactly.

628
00:55:03,892 --> 00:55:05,260
Scott: Those sorts of things can be detected.

629
00:55:05,400 --> 00:55:11,800
Scott: In the real world, then all you can do is just, like I said, for the procedures is vigilance, right?

630
00:55:12,140 --> 00:55:21,660
Scott: You have to be paying attention, carefully checking the game state against the legal game state to see if anything bad has happened.

631
00:55:21,880 --> 00:55:42,010
Scott: And depending on the design of the game, there could be situations where if the game is poorly written or poorly designed or has a flaw that no one recognized because even the best games aren't perfect, where you could follow all the procedures correctly and one rule could conflict another rule and following correct procedures puts the game in a state.

632
00:55:42,311 --> 00:55:45,220
Scott: that breaks another rule about what's a legal state.

633
00:55:47,101 --> 00:56:06,700
Rym: God, now I think back to, I don't know if it was a bad state or we just were unwilling to interrogate the rules to a conclusion, but I remember the one time since college that we played Diplomacy and we ended the game partly because a series of conflicts formed a perfect loop around the game board.

634
00:56:06,920 --> 00:56:07,912
Scott: I think there's an answer for that.

635
00:56:07,932 --> 00:56:08,498
Scott: we just didn't know.

636
00:56:08,660 --> 00:56:10,079
Rym: I think there is, but we couldn't figure it out.

637
00:56:11,901 --> 00:56:12,204
Scott: No.

638
00:56:12,547 --> 00:56:13,960
Scott: I'm sure the internet has the answer somewhere.

639
00:56:14,961 --> 00:56:16,880
Scott: Diplomacy is a well-trod ground.

640
00:56:17,140 --> 00:56:34,159
Rym: At least in tabletop games, what that really comes down to is a well-designed game provides enough avenues and information about transactions and state changes in the game to ensure that players who are paying attention can notice if something has happened that shouldn't have happened.

641
00:56:34,960 --> 00:56:47,660
Rym: Badly designed games will often leave opportunities where someone will not cheat on purpose, but someone who's not paying attention will just collect way too many victory points or pick up too many cards or have too many cards in their hand and no one notices.

642
00:56:48,541 --> 00:56:53,540
Rym: I think that's just good game design to avoid those kinds of ambiguous possibilities.

643
00:56:55,241 --> 00:57:10,886
Scott: I guess the final thing you can do in your actual life, most people listening are probably not game adjudicators, game designers maybe, is the thing that you could do in your actual life of game and sport playing to prevent cheating.

644
00:57:10,926 --> 00:57:15,840
Scott: the most is to simply refuse to play with a cheating person.

645
00:57:17,141 --> 00:57:28,080
Scott: If cheating people, if peoples don't play games with them, then that helps quelch the overall cheating that goes on in the world.

646
00:57:28,882 --> 00:57:37,780
Rym: This is why I'm totally okay with online ortho games, the aggressive perma-banning of people who cheat, I am 100% there for that, I wish they could be more aggressive.

647
00:57:38,600 --> 00:57:47,639
Scott: Yeah, it's like if your uncle is cheating and you don't have the authority and you can't stop him and no one cares, just stop playing, you don't have to play.

648
00:57:48,060 --> 00:57:54,940
Rym: And every Christmas when the family asks you to play Monopoly with the cheating uncle, just say no and remind everyone that he's a piece of shit.

649
00:57:55,523 --> 00:57:56,066
Scott: Yeah, just like.

650
00:57:56,147 --> 00:58:00,952
Scott: no, you guys cheat, I don't want to play with the cheating way, and you guys don't seem to care about the cheating.

651
00:58:00,972 --> 00:58:05,420
Scott: so you go play your cheating game and I'll stay over here with the pet and the dog, I don't know, right?

652
00:58:10,292 --> 00:58:12,550
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

653
00:58:12,770 --> 00:58:15,210
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

654
